<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-09" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Week 9</title>

<introduction>
    <p>
        This is an outline of the topics we covered in Week 9 of the semester, from October 26 to October 30.
    </p>
</introduction>

<handout component="cur">
    <title>Monday 10/26</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Solve rational equations
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Simplify rational functions
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Solve application problems using rational functions
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Solve radical equations
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Rational Functions</title>

<p component="b" workspace="4in">
   We started talking about rational equations and functions last week. What were the main ideas and points there?
</p>
<p component="a">
    We started talking about rational equations and functions last week. What were the main ideas and points there? Equations involving fractions. Solve them by clearing denominators. Always need to check for extraneous solutions.
</p>

    </paragraphs>
</page>

<page>
    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve <md>
                    1 + \frac{\frac{11}{3}}{x-4} = \frac{\frac{8}{3}}{x-1}
                </md>.
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>
</page>


<page>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Rational Expressions and Functions</title>

        <p component="b" workspace="2in">
            What if we have a rational function, something that looks like <md>
                f(x) = \frac{3}{x+2} - \frac{5x+1}{x-3}
            </md>. What can we do with that?  
            
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            What if we have a rational function, something that looks like <md>
                f(x) = \frac{3}{x+2} - \frac{5x+1}{x-3}
            </md>. What can we do with that? Need to get to a common denominator and leave that on the bottom. We don't have another side of the equation to clear things to. 
             
        </p>


        
    </paragraphs>
</page>

<page>
    <example workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Write <md>
                    f(x) = \frac{x-\frac{5}{2}}{x-6} + \frac{x - \frac{1}{2}}{x-2}
                </md> as a single fraction. Then, use this to determine the values of <m>x</m> where <m>f(x) = 1</m>.
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>
    
</page>

<page>
    <example workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Write <md>
                    f(x) = \frac{3}{x-1} - \frac{5}{x+3}
                </md> as a single fraction. Then, use this to determine the values of <m>x</m> where <m>f(x) = 2</m>.
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>
</page>

<page>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Applications of Rational Functions</title>
        
        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            Rational functions can also show up in a variety of applications.
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            Rational functions can also show up in a variety of applications. One of the main examples is speed and distance problems. In the past we had distance is rate times time. But, what if you know something about the rate and need to solve for the time? Then the variable ends up in the denominator.
        </p>

    </paragraphs>
</page>

</handout>

<handout component="cur">
    <title>Wednesday 10/28</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Solve radical equations.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Solve absolute value equations and inequalities.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
<page>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Radical Equations</title>
        
<p component="b" workspace="4in">
    The next type of equation we want to work on solving is radical equations.
</p>
<p component="a">
    The next type of equation we want to work on solving is radical equations. These are equations that involve a square root. We want to get rid of that square root to solve the equation, so we need to isolate the radical term, then square both sides of the equation. If <m>a=b</m>, then <m>a^2 = b^2</m>. Again need to check for extraneous solutions. 
</p>

    </paragraphs>
</page>
<page>
<example workspace="2in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Solve the equation <md>
                \sqrt{x-3} = 2
            </md>.
            
        </p>
    </statement>

</example>
<example workspace="2in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Solve the equation <md>
                \sqrt{4x+1} = 5
            </md>.
            
        </p>
    </statement>

</example>

</page>

<page>
    <example workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve the equation <md>
                    4 + \sqrt{2x+7} = x
                </md>.
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>
    
</page>


<page>
    <example workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve the equation <md>
                    6 + \sqrt{x+14} = x
                </md>.
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>
    
</page>

<page>

<p component="b" workspace="2in">
    Sometimes, this process isn't enough to eliminate all radicals from your equation.
</p>
<p component="a">
    Sometimes, this process isn't enough to eliminate all radicals from your equation. If not, reisolate the remaining radical and do it again.
</p>

</page>

<page>
    <example workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve the equation <md>
                    \sqrt{x+2} + \sqrt{x-3} = 5
                </md>.
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>
    
</page>

<page>
    <example workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve the equation <md>
                    \sqrt{x+5} + \sqrt{x-1} = 8
                </md>.
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>
    
</page>

<page>
    <example workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve the equation <md>
                    \sqrt{2x-1} + \sqrt{x+4} = 6
                </md>.
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>
    
</page>

<page>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Absolute Value Equations</title>
        
<p component="b" workspace="4in">
    The next type of equation we want to solve are equations involving absolute values. How can we handle these equations?
</p>
<p component="a">
    The next type of equation we want to solve are equations involving absolute values. How can we handle these equations? What does the absolute value mean? That leads to us splitting an equation into two parts. Do some examples.
</p>

    </paragraphs>
</page>

<page>
    <p component="b" workspace="4in">
        How can we summarize these methods?     
    </p>
    <p component="a">
        How can we summarize these methods?      
    </p>
</page>

<page>
    <example workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve the equation
                <md>
                    |2x+1| = 3
                </md>.
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>

    <example workspace="2in ">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve the equation 
                <md>
                    |x-1| + 4 = 2
                </md>.
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>
    
    
</page>

<page>
    <example workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve the equation 
                <md>
                    |3x-5| + 2 = 11
                </md>.
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>
    
</page>

<page>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Inequalities with Absolute Values</title>

        <p component="b" workspace="3in">
            What happens if these absolute value equalities are actually inequalities? How does that work?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            What happens if these absolute value equalities are actually inequalities? How does that work? Absolute value smaller means closer to zero, or a two-sided inequality. Absolute value bigger means farther from zero, or an <q>or</q> inequality.
        </p>
        
    </paragraphs>
</page>

<page>
    <example workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve
                <md>
                    |3x-2| \leq 4
                </md>.
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>
    
</page>

<page>
    <example workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve
                <md>
                    |2x+3| \gt 6
                </md>.
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>
    
</page>

<page>
    <example>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Solve each of the following inequalities.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\displaystyle |x-4| \geq 2</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\displaystyle |2x-5| \lt 3</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </example>
    
</page>


</handout>


<handout component="cur">
    <title>Friday 10/30</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Introduction to polynomials and polynomial modeling
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Polynomials</title>

<p component="b" workspace="3in">
    In Chapter 4, we will start dealing with general polynomials. These are an extention of linear and quadratic functions that we have discussed previously.
</p>
<p component="a">
    In Chapter 4, we will start dealing with general polynomials. These are an extention of linear and quadratic functions that we have discussed previously. Define polynomial. Degree, and leading term. List out first four degrees and what we call the polynomials.
</p>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            What kinds of functions are not polynomials?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            What kinds of functions are not polynomials? Absolute value, <m>1/x</m>, <m>\sqrt{x}</m>. 
        </p>
    </page>


    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Properties of Polynomials</title>
            
        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            There are some common properties shared by all polynomials.
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            There are some common properties shared by all polynomials. Sketch some graphs. Domain is all real numbers, continuous, smooth. Draw some non-polynomial graphs to see the comparisons.
        </p>
            
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>End Behavior of Polynomials</title>
            
        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            Another nice fact about polynomials is that they have predictable behavior at the left and right ends. The general rule is that the leading term determines the behavior.
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            Another nice fact about polynomials is that they have predictable behavior at the left and right ends. The general rule is that the leading term determines the behavior. What does this mean? Here is the graph of <m>x^4</m>. Then draw <m>x^4 + 3x^3 + ...</m>.
        </p>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <theorem xml:id="thm-leading-term-test">
            <statement>
                <p component="b" workspace="3in">
                    If <m>a_nx^n</m> is the leading term of a polynomial function <m>f(x)</m> then the graph of <m>f(x)</m> looks like:
                </p>
                <p component="a">
                    If <m>a_nx^n</m> is the leading term of a polynomial function <m>f(x)</m> then the graph of <m>f(x)</m> looks like: draw table of results.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </theorem>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Match the following polynomial functions to their graphs using the leading term test.
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f_1(x) = 2x^3 - x^2 - 5x + 1</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f_2(x) = -x^6+2x^4-x^3+x^2</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f_3(x) = x^4 + 2x^3- 2x^2-2x-1</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f_4(x) = -x^5 + 3x^3 - 2x  +1</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <sidebyside>
                    <image width="60%">
                <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="leading-test-graph-1">
            <diagram xmlns="https://prefigure.org" dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition> f(x)=x^4 + 2*x^3- 2*x^2-2*x-1 </definition>
  <coordinates bbox="[-6,-6, 6,6]">
    <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
    <graph at="f" function="f"/>
  </coordinates>
</diagram>
            </prefigure>
            </image>
            <image width="60%">
                <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="leading-test-graph-2">
            <diagram xmlns="https://prefigure.org" dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition> f(x)=-x^6+2*x^4-x^3+x^2</definition>
  <coordinates bbox="[-6,-6,6,6]">
    <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
    <graph at="f" function="f"/>
  </coordinates>
</diagram>
            </prefigure>
            </image>
                </sidebyside>
                <sidebyside>
                    <image width="60%">
                <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="leading-test-graph-3">
            <diagram xmlns="https://prefigure.org" dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition> f(x)=-x^5 + 3*x^3 - 2*x  +1 </definition>
  <coordinates bbox="[-6,-6,6,6]">
    <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
    <graph at="f" function="f"/>
  </coordinates>
</diagram>
            </prefigure>
            </image>
            <image width="60%">
                <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="leading-test-graph-4">
            <diagram xmlns="https://prefigure.org" dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition> f(x)=2*x^3 - x^2 - 5*x + 1 </definition>
  <coordinates bbox="[-6,-6,6,6]">
    <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
    <graph at="f" function="f"/>
  </coordinates>
</diagram>
            </prefigure>
            </image>
                </sidebyside>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>
</handout>


</section>